 {"id":"CF-182","title":"P3 — the PRD's requirement-id column and the board's task-id column share the CF-nnn namespace, so the milestone blocker list names CF-110/CF-112 that have never existed as rows and 'the milestone cannot be claimed while any row above is open' is undecidable for those two entries","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-04T08:32:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T08:32:00Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"88f6a7c404f4114e671713fc49da94219694567dd06f9a03dc0f54c7fea60f17"},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 by the code-factory-duckbrain-pm tick (07-36-30, third pass) from a fresh clone of origin/main at 93ef524. `docs/PRD-code-factory.md` opens a requirements table whose ID column runs CF-101, CF-102, ... (`grep -c '^| CF-1' docs/PRD-code-factory.md` = 12 at 93ef524). Those are REQUIREMENT ids: the board carries no row for them (`jq -r 'select(.id==\"CF-110\") | .id' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl | wc -l` = 0 and the same for CF-112). The milestone section then reuses the identical spelling for board rows: 'The open defects this milestone's legs depend on are CF-080 ..., the never-exercised legs CF-001 ..., CF-004 ..., CF-110 (no destroy path ships) and CF-112 (no dispatch-targets.jsonl consumer ships)' and closes with 'The milestone cannot be claimed while any row above is open.' A reader who runs the check the same section prescribes — `jq -r 'select(.status!=\"complete\") | [.id,.priority] | @tsv' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` — cannot find CF-110 or CF-112 at all: `grep -c 'CF-110' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` = 2 matches only inside other rows' prose, `jq -r 'select(.task_id==\"CF-110\") | .id' .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | wc -l` = 0, and `git log --all --oneline -S'\"id\":\"CF-110\"' -- .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` is empty, so no ref has ever carried such a row. The two spellings are indistinguishable by shape, so the milestone's own membership rule ('every open row whose failure stops one of the four legs ... must appear here') is not machine-decidable for exactly those two entries, and a future tick cannot tell a dangling blocker from a requirement. Distinct from every open row: `grep -c 'CF-182' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` = 0 before this row was filed, and CF-175 covers a different defect (the deployed lane prompt names a PRD path no ref carries), not the id namespace.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce at any tip from the repo root: the row's probe prints `req_ids_in_prd:` > 0, `cf110_row: 0`, `cf112_row: 0`, `cf110_events: 0`, `cited_in_milestone:` > 0 and exits 0. `git log --all --oneline -S'\"id\":\"CF-110\"' -- .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` is empty (no ref ever carried such a row).\n1. The Requirements preamble in `docs/PRD-code-factory.md` states that its ID column is a requirement namespace and NOT the board's task-id namespace, so a CF-nnn in that column need not exist as a row; `grep -c 'requirement namespace' docs/PRD-code-factory.md` >= 1.\n2. Every CF-nnn cited in the milestone blocker list resolves deterministically: for each id the check prints either `row=<status>` (from `jq` over the board) or `requirement-only` (from the requirements table), and it exits 0 only when every cited board row is non-complete and every requirement-only entry carries a testless `Verified by` cell. Ship the check as a committed script (e.g. `docs/audits/prd-blocker-resolution.sh`) so a stranger can re-run it; `bash <script>` exits 0 at the fix commit and exits nonzero when one cited id is deleted from both the board and the table (prove the negative by deleting one id in a scratch copy, not in the tree).\n3. A gate arm fails when the PRD cites a CF-nnn that resolves to neither namespace: add the case to `tests/selftest.sh` (or to the docs audit gate `docs/audits/cf099-prd-drift-probe.sh` if it is the shipped home for PRD checks), RED against the pre-fix PRD and GREEN after, with the row id in the arm's name.\n4. At the fix commit `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with rc 0 and `bash scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` (pre-existing free-form-vocabulary warnings excepted).","probe":"printf 'req_ids_in_prd: '; grep -c '^| CF-1' docs/PRD-code-factory.md; printf 'cf110_row: '; jq -r 'select(.id==\"CF-110\") | .id' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl | wc -l; printf 'cf112_row: '; jq -r 'select(.id==\"CF-112\") | .id' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl | wc -l; printf 'cf110_events: '; jq -r 'select(.task_id==\"CF-110\") | .id' .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | wc -l; printf 'cited_in_milestone: '; grep -c 'CF-110\\|CF-112' docs/PRD-code-factory.md; printf 'namespace_legend: '; grep -c 'requirement namespace' docs/PRD-code-factory.md; true"}
 {"id": "CF-183", "title": "P2 \u2014 install-hermes.sh certifies ANY `hermes` on PATH as the pinned harness: the version row never compares to $PIN, and --apply then reports the pin \"already installed\" and never creates the venv", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 3, "reasoning": "Measured 2026-11-04 by the code-factory-duckbrain-dogfood tick code-factory-duckbrain-dogfood-2026-11-04-08-40-09 from a fresh clone of origin/main at 202fb61. The adapter resolves its binary through `hb()` (scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:26), which is `[ -x \"$VENV/bin/hermes\" ] && echo \"$VENV/bin/hermes\" || have hermes` \u2014 so any `hermes` on PATH is accepted as the harness \u2014 and the version row (:60) prints `ok \"version\" \"$(\"$h\" --version | head -1)\"` with no comparison to PIN (:17, default 0.19.0). Arm K: a two-line PATH stub printing `Hermes Agent v9.9.9-not-the-pin` yields `PASS binary \u2014 <stub>/hermes` and `PASS version \u2014 Hermes Agent v9.9.9-not-the-pin` (`HERMES: FAIL (1)`, the only FAIL is the port row), so the one row that exists to identify the harness certifies a binary that is not the pin. Arm L: `--apply` with the same stub and a fresh HOME prints `.. already installed: Hermes Agent v9.9.9-not-the-pin` and creates no `$HOME/.hermes/venv` (`VENV_PRESENT=no`), so the pin is never installed and the operator is told it is. This inverts the promise AGENTS.md and specs/HARNESS.md make for the only measured adapter (\"an unmeasured adapter refuses --apply rather than pretending\"): the measured adapter passes and pretends. Distinct from CF-174 (the version row prints an EMPTY measurement when the binary exits non-zero), CF-141 (verdict counts FAIL rows only) and CF-125 (a typo'd --harness is indistinguishable from an unmeasured one): here the row PASSes on a non-empty value that contradicts the pin, and install/apply is skipped on that basis.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. Reproduce at any tip from a clone root: the row's probe prints `PASS  version \u2014 Hermes Agent v9.9.9-not-the-pin` today; after the fix the same command must print a named FAIL/WARN for the version row (e.g. `FAIL version \u2014 v9.9.9-not-the-pin != pin 0.19.0`) and must not print `PASS version`.\n2. The version row compares the measured version to `$PIN` (or to the version actually installed in `$VENV`) and fails on a mismatch; a `hermes` resolved outside `$VENV` is either refused or labelled host-provided, so a node cannot certify a binary it does not own.\n3. `--apply` on a host with no `$VENV/bin/hermes` creates the venv and installs `hermes-agent==$PIN` even when another `hermes` answers on PATH; the \"already installed\" branch is reachable only for a venv binary whose version equals the pin.\n4. `tests/selftest.sh` gains an arm that is RED against the current shape (a PATH stub whose version text is not the pin still prints `PASS version`) and GREEN after the fix; `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with rc 0.\n5. Any doc or spec sentence claiming this gate proves the pinned install (README status row 1, specs/HARNESS.md adapters section) is corrected in the same commit, per AGENTS.md rule 1.", "probe": "d=$(mktemp -d); printf '#!/bin/sh\\necho \"Hermes Agent v9.9.9-not-the-pin\"\\n' > \"$d/hermes\"; chmod +x \"$d/hermes\"; PATH=\"$d:$PATH\" HERMES_PYTHON_BIN=python3.12 scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh --check 2>&1 | grep -E 'binary|version|HERMES:'; h=$(mktemp -d); PATH=\"$d:$PATH\" HOME=\"$h\" HERMES_PYTHON_BIN=python3.12 scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh --apply 2>&1 | grep -E 'already installed|install'; test -d \"$h/.hermes/venv\" && echo VENV_PRESENT=yes || echo VENV_PRESENT=no; true", "created_at": "2026-11-04T08:43:54Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T08:43:54Z", "detail": {"tick": "code-factory-duckbrain-dogfood-2026-11-04-08-40-09", "lane": "code-factory-duckbrain-dogfood", "witness": "arms K and L"}}
 {"id": "CF-184", "title": "P4 \u2014 project-node.sh reports every missing optional tool as \"some repos' tests need node\", so jq and rg both tell the operator to install node", "status": "pending", "priority": "P4", "complexity": 1, "reasoning": "Measured 2026-11-04 by the code-factory-duckbrain-dogfood tick code-factory-duckbrain-dogfood-2026-11-04-08-40-09 from a fresh clone of origin/main at 202fb61. scripts/project-node.sh:208-209 loops `for t in jq rg node` and pairs all three with one hardcoded hint: `warn \"tool:$t\" \"missing (some repos' tests need node)\"`. Arm M2 reproduced it without a bunker node, from a curated PATH that symlinks every tool layer 10 needs and deliberately omits jq, rg and node (each confirmed present outside the curated PATH): the layer prints `WARN tool:jq \u2014 missing (some repos' tests need node)` and `WARN tool:rg \u2014 missing (some repos' tests need node)` and still ends `NODE: PASS` rc 0. The layer whose only job is to tell an operator what to install names a different package than the tool it names, twice. Same class measured live on bunker-mvp in an earlier fire (layer 10 of a real node create). Distinct from CF-172 (the layer-40 remedy names a --repo flag that was already supplied): that is the remedy for a MISSING repo, this is the hint for a MISSING tool, and neither line knows which tool it is talking about.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. Reproduce at any tip from a clone root: the row's probe prints three identical hint lines today (`tool:jq`, `tool:rg`, `tool:node` all carrying `some repos' tests need node`).\n2. Each tool's WARN names its own package (`apt install jq`, `ripgrep`, `nodejs`) or a per-tool table replaces the hardcoded string; no row may tell the operator to install a different tool than the one it names.\n3. `tests/selftest.sh` gains an arm that fails if the three hints are identical again (loop over `jq|rg|node` asserting each hint mentions the tool's own name); RED against the current shape, GREEN after.\n4. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with rc 0.", "probe": "grep -n -A1 'for t in jq rg node' scripts/project-node.sh; c=$(mktemp -d); for t in bash sh awk sed grep cat head tail tr cut sort wc uname nproc df mktemp rm mkdir ls id date dirname basename find stat env chmod printf python3 git curl tar sha256sum hostname; do p=$(command -v \"$t\") && ln -sf \"$p\" \"$c/$t\"; done; PATH=\"$c\" bash ./scripts/project-node.sh --check --layers 10 --project hintarm 2>&1 | grep -E 'tool:|NODE:'; true", "created_at": "2026-11-04T08:43:54Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T08:43:54Z", "detail": {"tick": "code-factory-duckbrain-dogfood-2026-11-04-08-40-09", "lane": "code-factory-duckbrain-dogfood", "witness": "arm M2"}}
+{"id":"CF-185","title":"P3 — add_family_lanes.py has no read-only mode and no overridable target: --help either PUTs the live scheduler or dies at import on /proc/<pid>/environ","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 by the code-factory-duckbrain-qa tick 2026-11-04-09-00-46 at origin/main 171bf23 (selftest PASS, parity 0/0). scripts/add_family_lanes.py is the shipped loader for the lane families, and it reads argv nowhere: `grep -cE 'argparse|sys\\.argv|--help|--check|--dry-run' scripts/add_family_lanes.py` = 0, so every invocation — including `--help` — runs the module-level write path. The write surfaces are live and neither is overridable: `API = \"http://127.0.0.1:9090\"` (line 14) and `DB = ~/.hermes/coding-hermes/scheduler.db` (line 15) are literals with no environment seam (the file's only `environ` occurrence is the /proc read), and the module then (a) POSTs /api/v1/projects per lane and PUTs its namespace_id (lines 70-82), and (b) opens the live DB and runs `UPDATE projects SET parent=..., admission_mode='cooldown', prompt=<file>, prompt_mode='append'` unconditionally for all 12 lanes (lines 87-98). Its operator credential is harvested at import: `open(f\"/proc/{PID}/environ\", \"rb\")` (line 27) over the MainPID of the `coding-hermes-scheduler` user unit, so on any host where that unit is absent, stopped, or owned by another user the script dies with an unhandled FileNotFoundError before any argument is parsed, and on this host that file IS readable (scheduler_MainPID=1375980, environ_readable=yes), so `--help` would rewrite the live family rather than describe it. Probe output at 171bf23: argv_handling=0 / api_base=API = \"http://127.0.0.1:9090\" / api_write_calls=1 / FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: '/proc/0/environ' / rc=1. Two further host couplings in the same file: ROOT is hardcoded to ~/code-factory, so the loader cannot be run from a clone of the repo it ships in, and the per-lane prompt is read from `{ROOT}/prompts/{name}.md`. AGENTS.md conventions ('no root, ever', `--check|--apply`, idempotence) and every other shipped provisioner (node-create.sh, project-node.sh, bunker-server-install.sh) carry a read-only mode; this one does not. Distinct from CF-170 (load_lane_config.py) because that hazard is containable with SCHEDULER_DB=<copy> and its writes are DB-only, while this script's write set is the live scheduler HTTP API plus the live DB and its foreign-host failure mode is an import-time traceback rather than a silent rewrite.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree from the repo root: the probe below prints `argv_handling=0`, `api_base=API = \"http://127.0.0.1:9090\"`, `api_write_calls=1`, a `FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: '/proc/0/environ'` line and `rc=1` — the script accepts no read-only form and refuses uncleanly when the scheduler MainPID cannot be resolved.\n1. Read-only mode is the DEFAULT and provably writes nothing: with no argument, `--check`, `--dry-run` or `--help`, the script prints the intended per-lane changes (name, workdir, namespace, prompt source) and issues zero POST/PUT/PATCH requests and zero SQL writes; the mutating form is an explicit `--apply`. Proven against a fixture target, not the live one: the script gains an API-base and DB override (documented env vars) so a `--check` run against a local recording HTTP stub and a scratch DB copy records 0 writes and prints the plan, while an `--apply` run against the same fixture records the same writes the live path would issue.\n2. The credential is explicit: the script resolves the operator token from a declared environment variable or a mode-600 token file with a named failure (exit 2, a `FAIL token — <reason>` line, no traceback) and NEVER from `/proc/<pid>/environ` of another process; a run with the token absent prints that refusal rather than the current `FileNotFoundError`, and `grep -c '/proc/' scripts/add_family_lanes.py` prints 0.\n3. The host path is no longer baked in: the repo root comes from the script's own location or a documented env override, so the loader runs from a clone of this repo and the per-lane prompt it reads is that clone's `prompts/<lane>.md` (the run prints the resolved root and prompt paths it used).\n4. A gate can fail on this: `tests/selftest.sh` carries a numbered arm that runs the fixed script in read-only mode against a fixture stub and asserts zero recorded writes plus the printed plan, and its red arm at the pre-fix tip is named in the output (the pre-fix run either records a write or dies at import).\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` and `bash scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` at the fix commit, and the fix is pushed with `git rev-list --count origin/main..HEAD` = 0.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$d/bin\"; printf '#!/bin/sh\\necho 0\\n' > \"$d/bin/systemctl\"; chmod +x \"$d/bin/systemctl\"; echo \"argv_handling=$(grep -cE 'argparse|sys\\.argv|--help|--check|--dry-run' scripts/add_family_lanes.py)\"; echo \"api_base=$(grep -m1 -E '^API = ' scripts/add_family_lanes.py)\"; echo \"api_write_calls=$(grep -cE 'api\\(\"(POST|PUT|PATCH)\"' scripts/add_family_lanes.py)\"; PATH=\"$d/bin:$PATH\" python3 scripts/add_family_lanes.py --help 2>&1 | tail -2; echo \"rc=${PIPESTATUS[0]}\"; rm -rf \"$d\"","created_at":"2026-11-04T09:07:54Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T09:07:54Z"}
